From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Julien Etienne <julien.etienne@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 09:42:13 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] soc: mediatek: mt6797-spm-sleep: run one SODI period on
 demand

Stage D3 of the cpuidle record: a debugfs file, sodi_once_ms, runs the
vendor SODI program once on CPU0 while the system is otherwise awake,
ended by the PCM timer after 1-2000 ms. It reuses the suspend path's
loader, PCM reset, kick, wake read-back and restore, now one function
for both programs, with what differs (program, SPM_SRC_REQ,
SPM_SRC2_MASK, WFI enables, flags, timer, wrapper slots) in a scenario.

The SODI program, pcm_sodi.bin from the owner's vendor files, is
hash-pinned and copied into the carveout after the suspend program; it
is optional. Its flags are the suspend ones without bit 7, which pcm_sodi
reads with another meaning, and with DIS_VCORE_DVS, so it issues no PMIC
wrapper command; CG mode (DRAM self-refresh only) unless sodi_pdn is set.
For the wait the local timer's interrupt is masked, so CPU0 stays in WFI,
and I2C6 is locked, since the program soft-resets it. The period is
refused unless AP DMA and USB are clock-gated, as SODI does not honour
their DRAM requests. DUMMY1_PWR_CON and SPM_PASR_DPD_0, which the program
uses, are logged; PASR_DPD_0 is restored.

Local experiment, depends on a non-free program the project does not ship.
---
 drivers/soc/mediatek/mt6797-spm-sleep.c | 247 ++++++++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 212 insertions(+), 35 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/soc/mediatek/mt6797-spm-sleep.c b/drivers/soc/mediatek/mt6797-spm-sleep.c
